 Sessions, Auto-Forwarding, and Partitions

## Session Overview
Sometimes messages need to be delivered in order — e.g., items must be added to a user's shopping cart in the same order as added, otherwise the user would think the system is broken. That's what **sessions** are for.

## Sessions Overview
This feature gives per-key ordering. Related messages are tagged with a **Session ID** (e.g., all updates for the same order). Service Bus delivers one session to exactly one consumer at a time, keeping those messages in sequence. While processing a session, the consumer can keep lightweight state and resume exactly where it left off if the worker restarts. The result: clean, per-customer first-in-first-out processing without sacrificing horizontal scale across many concurrent sessions.

## Dead-Lettering and Auto-Forwarding
When a message can't be completed (e.g., it exceeded its delivery count or couldn't be parsed), Service Bus moves it to the **dead-letter queue** — keeping the main path unblocked and the backlog healthy. **Auto-forwarding** lets you pipe dead-lettered messages to a separate queue or a topic subscription — fanning problem messages out to a diagnostics flow, capturing them for audit, or triggering a retry queue for reprocessing, without adding custom rerouting code to primary consumers.

## Duplicate Detection
Network connections can break, causing clients to time out and resend a message — doing the work twice could be problematic (e.g., you don't want to be double-charged). Enabling **duplicate detection** with a time window helps: each message carries an ID, and if Service Bus sees the same ID again within that window, it discards the duplicate — ensuring the message is processed once, even when the network isn't cooperating.

## Partitions for High Throughput
**Partitioning** improves availability and throughput. A partitioned queue or topic spreads messages across multiple brokers under the hood, so several flows of messages are processed in parallel — like having multiple lanes on a highway instead of just one. This also improves resiliency: one node can fail without taking the entity down. Consumers still use the same API and connection string — Service Bus manages the distribution behind the scenes.

## Putting It All Together
A robust pattern combines: **sessions** to preserve order where it matters, **dead-lettering plus auto-forwarding** to quarantine and route problem messages, **duplicate detection** to prevent rework, and **partitioning** to keep the whole system fast and highly available.
